\section{What these atlases are for}{

These atlases are built for visualising results.
They are there to give general anatomical guidance — roughly where in the brain an effect sits, and which structures neighbour it.
They are not a source of precision geometry.
Nothing here is meant to be measured from:
not volumes, not surface areas, not distances, and not the exact boundary between two neighbouring parcels.
An analysis that needs those numbers should take them from the annotation, surface or volume the atlas was built from, never from the atlas.

That positioning buys the pipelines one specific kind of slack, and no more.
Acceptable by design:
vertices that sit a millimetre or so from where the source data puts them,
shape lost to mesh smoothing and decimation,
corners rounded off by polygon simplification (the \code{keep} argument of \code{\link[=atlas_simplify]{atlas_simplify()}} and \code{\link[=atlas_polish]{atlas_polish()}}),
and the difference between an atlas's own silhouette and the same parcellation resampled onto a shared surface.
Not acceptable, and not excused by it:
anything categorically wrong.
A region drawn on the wrong hemisphere,
a label attached to the wrong structure,
or a region missing from a view where it is anatomically present
is a bug, however tight the tolerance on its vertices.
A tool that puts the left hippocampus on the right is not being imprecise;
it is giving confident wrong guidance, which is worse than giving none.
Please report those as bugs.
}

\section{What a 3D plot shows}{

Cortical and cerebellar atlases do not carry their own 3D geometry.
They store vertex indices.
A cortical atlas records, for each region, which vertices of the fsaverage5 surface it claims (0 to 10241 per hemisphere);
a cerebellar atlas records which vertices of the SUIT surface it claims (0 to 28934).
Rendering either in 3D therefore draws fsaverage5's or SUIT's anatomy with the atlas's labels painted onto it.
The cortical shape of the brain the parcellation came from is never shown, and cannot be.
Registration decides only which vertices each region claims.

Subcortical and white-matter tract atlases are different.
Those carry a real mesh per structure, tessellated from the source volume or from streamlines, so the 3D geometry is their own.
A cerebellar atlas built from a volume carries meshes for its deep nuclei for the same reason:
those sit off the flatmap surface.
}

\section{Getting started}{

\code{sitrep()} reports whether this machine has what each pipeline needs.
Run it first — most pipelines want FreeSurfer, and some want Connectome
Workbench or an optional R package.

Pick the creator that matches the input you have:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Cortical surface} — \code{\link[=create_cortical_from_annotation]{create_cortical_from_annotation()}} for a
FreeSurfer \code{.annot}, \code{\link[=create_cortical_from_gifti]{create_cortical_from_gifti()}} or
\code{\link[=create_cortical_from_cifti]{create_cortical_from_cifti()}} for surface formats,
\code{\link[=create_cortical_from_labels]{create_cortical_from_labels()}} for hand-drawn \code{.label} files, and
\code{\link[=create_cortical_from_neuromaps]{create_cortical_from_neuromaps()}} for a neuromaps annotation.
\item \strong{Subcortical} — \code{\link[=create_subcortical_from_volume]{create_subcortical_from_volume()}} from a segmentation
volume such as FreeSurfer's \code{aseg}.
\item \strong{Cerebellar} — \code{\link[=create_cerebellar_from_gifti]{create_cerebellar_from_gifti()}},
\code{\link[=create_cerebellar_from_annotation]{create_cerebellar_from_annotation()}} or
\code{\link[=create_cerebellar_from_volume]{create_cerebellar_from_volume()}}, all onto the SUIT flatmap.
\item \strong{White-matter tract} — \code{\link[=create_tract_from_tractography]{create_tract_from_tractography()}} from
streamlines, or \code{\link[=create_tract_from_volume]{create_tract_from_volume()}} from a tract label map.
\item \strong{Whole brain at once} — \code{\link[=create_wholebrain_from_volume]{create_wholebrain_from_volume()}}, which splits
one volume across the three pipelines above.
}

A freshly built atlas usually carries more vertices than a plot needs.
\code{\link[=atlas_polish]{atlas_polish()}} simplifies and rounds it off in one call;
\code{\link[=atlas_simplify]{atlas_simplify()}}, \code{\link[=atlas_smooth]{atlas_smooth()}} and \code{\link[=atlas_dilate]{atlas_dilate()}} are the separate
steps. \code{\link[=count_vertices]{count_vertices()}} is the number to watch.

To ship an atlas as its own package, \code{\link[=setup_atlas_repo]{setup_atlas_repo()}} scaffolds the
repository and \code{\link[=use_atlas_github_actions]{use_atlas_github_actions()}} adds its workflows.
}

